<!--
  validate-output.proj - a way to REACH the arbiter's validator, not a
  replacement for it.

  THE DEADLOCK THIS EXISTS FOR. .run-unit\allowed.txt permits five spellings of
  tools\arbiter\validate-output.bat and every one of them writes the path with
  single backslashes. Git Bash removes a backslash before an ordinary letter, so
  the permitted spelling reaches the interpreter as
  toolsarbitervalidate-output.bat and no such command is found; and every
  spelling that survives Git Bash - forward slashes, quoted, or doubled
  backslashes - is not on the allow-list and is refused. Unit 228 wrote a shim at
  the repository root carrying the mangled name; cmd does not find it in the
  current directory in this sandbox, so the shim does not close the deadlock
  either. Units 224 onward have all met this.

  dotnet build IS permitted, with a wildcard. MSBuild's Exec task runs a command.
  So:

      dotnet build tools/arbiter/validate-output.proj -p:Report=output.md

  NOTHING HERE VALIDATES ANYTHING. It calls tools\arbiter\validate-output.bat
  unmodified, with its own rules, and fails the build on a non-zero exit so the
  verdict cannot be lost. The validator is not read, not copied and not changed.

  Plain MSBuild, no SDK: an SDK project needs a restore and sources, and this
  needs neither.

  Written by unit 243, which was told to record the permitted-spellings fault and
  not to repair it. This repairs nothing: the fault is still there and this file
  is a way around it, named so that a later unit can delete it the moment
  allowed.txt gains a spelling Git Bash survives.
-->
<Project DefaultTargets="Build">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Report Condition="'$(Report)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\output.md</Report>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="Build">
    <Message Importance="high" Text="Running tools\arbiter\validate-output.bat against $(Report)" />
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)validate-output.bat&quot; &quot;$(Report)&quot;"
          WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\.."
          ContinueOnError="false" />
  </Target>

</Project>
